{
  "slug": "madhyamakakarika",
  "title": "Nagarjuna's Verses on the Middle Way",
  "originalTitle": "मूलमध्यमककारिका",
  "transliteration": "Mūlamadhyamakakārikā",
  "altTitles": [
    "Madhyamakakārikā",
    "Mūlamadhyamakakārikā",
    "Fundamental Verses on the Middle Way"
  ],
  "tradition": "buddhism",
  "school": "",
  "genre": "Philosophy",
  "textType": "Philosophical verses with a commentary",
  "category": "Mahayana",
  "subcategory": "Madhyamaka",
  "originalLanguage": "sa",
  "dateStart": 150,
  "dateEnd": 250,
  "dateDisplay": "c. 2nd–3rd century CE",
  "authorshipStatus": "traditional",
  "author": "nagarjuna",
  "attributionNote": "Ascribed to Nāgārjuna, the philosopher of the Madhyamaka school and, in the Tibetan tradition, the founder of its lineage; the verses are the root text of the school and were commented on by Buddhapālita, Bhāviveka and Candrakīrti. GRETIL's e-text was input by the Digital Sanskrit Buddhist Canon Input Project.",
  "description": "The Mūlamadhyamakakārikā examines what it is for anything to exist: causation and the four conditions, motion and rest, the senses and their objects, the arising and enduring of what is conditioned, agents and actions, fire and fuel, the self, the twelvefold chain of dependent arising, the tathāgata and the noble truths, and finally nirvana, which Nāgārjuna shows cannot be either an existing thing or a different one. Its twenty-seven chapters work by refutation rather than by proof, and its verses on emptiness as dependent arising became the standard of Buddhist philosophy in Tibet and East Asia.",
  "historicalContext": "Nāgārjuna's verses reorganised the Buddha's teaching on dependent arising into a systematic critique of the schools of his own day; with the commentaries they founded the Madhyamaka and the whole Tibetan curriculum of Buddhist philosophy, and they are read in the West as one of the sharpest works of ancient dialectic.",
  "availabilityNote": "Complete as GRETIL publishes it: the root verses of all twenty-seven chapters, 448 passages, streamed from GRETIL at read time, one chapter at a time. This is the original Sanskrit of the root text; the commentaries are not part of this file.",
